# NSW
g(name="Hunter Valley", sub="Zone", state="New South Wales", zone="Hunter Valley", styles=["white", "red"], dom="white",
  grapes="Semillon, Shiraz, Chardonnay, Verdelho", summary="The zone containing the Hunter region and its subregions. 'Hunter Valley' is the zone's registered name, while the region inside it is simply 'Hunter'; on labels both are used.", terroir="", producers=["Tyrrell's", "Mount Pleasant", "Brokenwood"], nerd="The zone 'Hunter Valley' and the region 'Hunter' have almost identical boundaries.", contains=["Hunter"])

g(name="Hunter", sub="Region", state="New South Wales", zone="Hunter Valley", styles=["white", "red", "sparkling"], dom="white",
  grapes="Semillon, Shiraz, Chardonnay, Verdelho",
  summary="The Hunter is Australia's oldest continuously producing wine region and its most counter-intuitive one. It is too hot, too humid and too wet at harvest for classic viticulture, yet it makes a white that exists nowhere else: Hunter Semillon, picked early at about 10 to 11 percent alcohol, fermented in stainless steel without oak, and bottled lean and lemony. Over ten to twenty years in bottle it develops toast, honey and lanolin flavours that taste as if it had been oaked. Its medium-bodied, earthy Shiraz, once called 'Hunter River Burgundy', is equally individual.\n\nJames Busby planted at Kirkton near Branxton around 1830; George Wyndham's Dalwood (1828), Henry Lindeman's Cavendish (1843) and Edward Tyrrell's Ashmans (1858) followed. Maurice O'Shea at Mount Pleasant (1921 to 1956) made the region's greatest mid-century wines, and his sons-in-spirit at Lindemans, Tyrrell's, Drayton's and McWilliam's kept the style alive. Since the 1970s, Lake's Folly (Max Lake's Cabernet, 1963), Brokenwood (Graveyard Shiraz) and the Tyrrells' Vat 1 Semillon and Vat 47 Chardonnay (Australia's first commercial Chardonnay varietal, 1971) have built its modern reputation.\n\nSummer rain and humid cloud cover make the Hunter warm but not baking: the cloud reduces sunshine, and harvest comes early, usually January, to beat the rain. Old, unirrigated vineyards on the red clay of the Brokenback Range (Pokolbin) give the Shiraz; sandy alluvial flats along the creeks give the best Semillon.",
  terroir="About 2,215 growing degree days but 556 mm of growing-season rain and frequent cloud. Two soil types matter: friable sandy alluvial flats (Semillon) and red-brown volcanic and limestone-derived clay loams on the slopes of the Brokenback Range (Shiraz).",
  producers=["Tyrrell's", "Mount Pleasant", "Lake's Folly", "Brokenwood", "Thomas Wines", "Meerea Park", "Keith Tulloch", "Andrew Thomas", "Silkman", "Pepper Tree", "Bimbadgen", "Margan", "First Creek", "Leogate"],
  nerd="Tyrrell's 4 Acres Shiraz comes from vines planted in 1879; the Old Patch block dates to 1867.", contains=["Pokolbin", "Broke Fordwich", "Upper Hunter Valley"])

g(name="Pokolbin", sub="Subregion", state="New South Wales", zone="Hunter Valley", styles=["white", "red"], dom="white",
  grapes="Semillon, Shiraz, Chardonnay",
  summary="The historic heart of the Lower Hunter, registered as a subregion in 2010, under the Brokenback Range west of Cessnock. Almost every famous Hunter name is here: Tyrrell's, Mount Pleasant, Lake's Folly, Brokenwood, Drayton's and Tulloch.",
  terroir="Red volcanic and limestone-derived clay loams on the slopes, sandy alluvial flats near the creeks; about 1,355 ha of vines in 173 square kilometres.", producers=["Tyrrell's", "Mount Pleasant", "Brokenwood", "Lake's Folly", "Tulloch", "Drayton's Family Wines"], nerd="Mount Pleasant's Lovedale vineyard, planted by O'Shea in 1946, gives one of the Hunter's longest-lived Semillons.", contains=[])

