g(name="South West Australia", sub="Zone", state="Western Australia", zone="South West Australia", styles=["red", "white"], dom="red", grapes="Cabernet Sauvignon, Chardonnay, Shiraz, Sauvignon Blanc, Semillon", summary="The zone covering Western Australia's fine-wine heartland: Margaret River, Great Southern, Geographe, Pemberton, Manjimup and Blackwood Valley, with about 9,900 ha of vines.", terroir="Maritime, Mediterranean.", producers=["Leeuwin Estate", "Cullen", "Howard Park"], nerd="", contains=["Margaret River", "Great Southern", "Geographe", "Pemberton", "Manjimup", "Blackwood Valley"])

g(name="Margaret River", sub="Region", state="Western Australia", zone="South West Australia", styles=["red", "white"], dom="red",
  grapes="Cabernet Sauvignon, Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Semillon, Shiraz, Merlot",
  summary="Margaret River is a narrow, wave-beaten cape between the Indian and Southern oceans, 270 km south of Perth, and in barely 50 years it has become one of the world's best regions for Cabernet Sauvignon and Chardonnay. Its existence was predicted on paper. In 1965 the agronomist John Gladstones published an analysis showing that the area's climate resembled Bordeaux in a dry year. Dr Tom Cullity, a Perth cardiologist, planted Vasse Felix in 1967; other doctors followed with Moss Wood (Bill Pannell, 1969) and Cullen (Kevin and Diana Cullen, 1971), then Cape Mentelle (David Hohnen, 1970), Sandalford, Leeuwin Estate (Denis and Tricia Horgan, 1974, with advice from Robert Mondavi) and Voyager.\n\nThe Cabernet is the region's signature: ripe, structured and fine-grained, with blackcurrant, bay leaf and a saline freshness, and with remarkable consistency from year to year because the ocean buffers both heat and frost. Chardonnay rivals it: Leeuwin Estate's Art Series Chardonnay, first made in 1980, was the first Australian white to rank with grand cru Burgundy, and the region's distinctive Gingin clone, with its 'hen and chicken' bunches of large and small berries, gives concentrated, pineapple-and-grapefruit wines. Semillon-Sauvignon Blanc blends are the everyday white.\n\nGladstones later proposed six subregions based on soils and climate: Yallingup, Carbunup, Wilyabrup, Treeton, Wallcliffe and Karridale. They are not registered GIs but are widely used. Wilyabrup, in the north-centre, holds Vasse Felix, Moss Wood, Cullen and Woodlands and is the classic Cabernet district; the southern Wallcliffe and Karridale, cooler and wetter, give finer Chardonnay.",
  terroir="Wine Australia records about 1,900 growing degree days and only 213 mm of rain from October to April against 914 mm for the year. Ocean on three sides prevents frost and extreme heat; strong winds at flowering cut yields. Soils are ancient: gravelly and sandy loams derived from granite and gneiss of the Leeuwin Complex, often lateritic, over clay.",
  producers=["Leeuwin Estate", "Vasse Felix", "Cullen", "Moss Wood", "Cape Mentelle", "Voyager Estate", "Woodlands", "Xanadu", "Pierro", "Deep Woods", "Fraser Gallop", "Howard Park", "Juniper", "Stella Bella", "Domaine Naturaliste", "Victory Point", "Cloudburst"],
  nerd="Cullen was certified biodynamic in 2004, and its Diana Madeline Cabernet blend is named after founder Di Cullen.",
  contains=["Yallingup (informal)", "Carbunup (informal)", "Wilyabrup (informal)", "Treeton (informal)", "Wallcliffe (informal)", "Karridale (informal)"])

g(name="Great Southern", sub="Region", state="Western Australia", zone="South West Australia", styles=["white", "red", "sparkling"], dom="red",
  grapes="Riesling, Shiraz, Cabernet Sauvignon, Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Pinot Noir",
  summary="Australia's largest wine region by area (about 150 km by 100 km) and one of its least known, the Great Southern covers granite hills, karri forest and coast in the far south-west. Its five subregions (Albany, Denmark, Frankland River, Mount Barker and Porongurup) range from maritime to continental, so the region can make both pristine Riesling and spicy Shiraz. It was the first part of the south-west to be planted for fine wine: after Harold Olmo of UC Davis recommended it in 1956, the state government planted a trial vineyard at Forest Hill near Mount Barker in 1966, and Forest Hill's 1975 Riesling won national trophies.",
  terroir="About 1,735 growing degree days, cool and dry in summer. Soils are mostly sandy and gravelly loams over granite, gneiss and laterite.", producers=["Plantagenet", "Frankland Estate", "Howard Park", "Castle Rock", "Forest Hill", "Larry Cherubino", "Singlefile", "Duke's Vineyard", "Ferngrove"], nerd="Alkoomi, at Frankland River, was planted in 1971 by Merv and Judy Lange on a sheep farm.", contains=["Albany", "Denmark", "Frankland River", "Mount Barker", "Porongurup"])

