# Tasmania, Queensland, territories
g(name="Tasmania", sub="Region", state="Tasmania", zone="Tasmania", styles=["sparkling", "red", "white"], dom="sparkling",
  grapes="Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, Sauvignon Blanc, Pinot Gris, Riesling",
  summary="Tasmania is one GI that works as a state, zone and region at once, and it has become the most dynamic fine-wine area in Australia. With fewer than 800 growing degree days on Wine Australia's figures, it is the only part of the country with a climate truly comparable to Champagne or Burgundy, and its 1,700 ha now supply most of Australia's best traditional-method sparkling and a growing share of its best Pinot Noir and Chardonnay. Large mainland companies (Accolade, Treasury, Brown Brothers) bought Tasmanian vineyards and wineries from the 1990s to secure cool-climate fruit.\n\nThe island's informal areas differ sharply. The north, the Tamar Valley and Pipers River, is cooler and wetter; Pipers River, close to Bass Strait, gives the tightest sparkling base (Jansz, Pipers Brook). The south-east, in the rain shadow of the central highlands, is drier and sunnier: the Coal River Valley around Richmond and Cambridge (Tolpuddle, Domaine A, Frogmore Creek, Pooley) and the Derwent Valley (Stefano Lubiana, Moorilla, Derwent Estate) make riper Pinot and Chardonnay. The East Coast around Swansea and Freycinet is warm by Tasmanian standards, and the Huon Valley and D'Entrecasteaux Channel in the far south are the coolest of all.\n\nEd Carr's House of Arras, with sparkling wines aged up to a decade or more on lees, and Jansz, the Yalumba-owned house founded with Louis Roederer in 1985, are the sparkling references; Tolpuddle, bought in 2011 by Martin Shaw and Michael Hill Smith of Shaw + Smith, Stefano Lubiana (biodynamic), Dawson James and Sailor Seeks Horse lead for Pinot and Chardonnay.",
  terroir="Cool and maritime, with long, slow autumns. Wine Australia records about 790 growing degree days and 585 mm of growing-season rain (the north is wetter, the south-east drier). Soils include red basalt-derived krasnozems at Pipers River, dolerite and sandstone in the south, and alluvial gravels along the Tamar and Derwent.",
  producers=["House of Arras", "Jansz", "Tolpuddle", "Stefano Lubiana", "Pipers Brook", "Josef Chromy", "Freycinet", "Domaine A", "Dawson James", "Sailor Seeks Horse", "Bangor", "Pooley", "Clover Hill", "Devil's Corner", "Holm Oak"],
  nerd="Tasmania's oldest wine estate in operation, Providence near Launceston, began in 1956 as La Provence; its French name was changed after a challenge from the French appellation authorities.",
  contains=["Tamar Valley (informal)", "Pipers River (informal)", "North West (informal)", "East Coast (informal)", "Coal River Valley (informal)", "Derwent Valley (informal)", "Huon Valley (informal)"])

